Subject:  Imprisonment as Quarantine

>From: "Kathleen Cadmus" <kcadmus@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
>Subject: No More Prisons
>
>From my own perspective, everyone has some non-zero
>probability of future crimes against me. Should I imprison you all to keep
>myself safe from you? Maybe only those with such a probability beyond a
>given threshold level?

Good challenge.  First, I think "quarantine" is the only justification
for imprisonment that withstands scrutiny.  The others are traditionally
used, but fail.  I'm not sure about retribution.  It seems like it
might be a good last resort if all else fails.

Two legal concepts would apply here, in my mind: that the law doesn't
deal with trifles, and unreasonable risk.  I would argue that it's
justifiable to "quarantine" people who present an unreasonable risk
to others.

By "quarantine," I mean something like "isolate to protect others from
danger in proportion to the risk and with the least possible difficulty
for the subject."  By "unreasonable," I mean something like "greater
than usual."  Call 'em fuzzy, analogical, ordinal measurements if you
like, but that's all we have to go on when it comes to these things.

Thus, I would say that trivial things like the way we breathe out
carbon monoxide exhaust wouldn't be actionable, but that we certainly
have the right to imprison people who are smallpox vectors or who
are reasonably proven threats to people.

>(Is this the justification for locking up a drunk
>driver despite the lack of demonstrable harm done?)

Unreasonable risk, yes, except that blood alcohol content is a poor
indicator of such risk.  Impairment testing is better.

>We are on the
>slipperiest of slopes here.

Not if we incorporate the limits I've described, as well as the others
that have evolved out of jurisprudence.

>Sorry but we all need to accept the responsibility of protecting our selves
>and our possessions *without* resorting to the coercive acts which we claim
>to deplore.

I'd exempt these pre-emptive strikes against unreasonable risk from the category
of coercion.

