Date:     Sat, 8 Jan 94 10:13:09 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  FAQ: Extropian Epistemology

In a post to the local SF Bay Area extropians list, DVDT asked about
what he might include in the FAQ his BATF is developing about "Extropian
Epistemology."  He indicated that he might describe extropians as
pan-critical rationalists, but did me the honor of naming me as a
notable dissenter from that position.  He indicated that he was
considering whether to state that there is some significant controversy
over what epistemology is extropian.  I think it's a bad idea to commit
extropianism to pan-critical rationalism (or any other epistemology) to
the exclusion of all others for reasons both general and specific.

General: epistemology is one of the least developed fields of knowledge,
and committing extropianism to any current view runs a high risk of
making it obsolete if and when the state of the art advances.  I would
object to committing extropianism to physics theories such as those
of Einstein's theories which currently seem correct for the same reason;
not that either are known to be incorrect (or incomplete), but to
allow for expansion beyond the boundaries of present knowledge about
the theory of knowledge and methods of acquiring it.

Specific: I would reject the descriptor "absolutism" DVDT attached to
my stance as inadequately descriptive of it and the epistemological
controversy I'm associated with in the extropian community in favor
of the term: "foundationalist."  The fundamental controversy seems
to be foundationalist v. anti-foundationalist, with the extropian
advocates of pan-critical rationalism taking the anti-foundationalist
position.

That said, one of the most prominent pan-critical rationalists, WW
Bartley III, seems to indicate that he might find some form of
foundationalism compatible with his views:

"Crucial to the presentationalist argument are, then, two things: the
desire to give a firm foundation or justification to the tenets of
science, and the construal of sense experience as the incorrigible
source of all knowledge...

"One way to escape the presentationalist trap is to give up the aim of
justifying one's knowledge.  This possibility is the focus of discussion
in the second part of this book.  Another way - also correct - is to
abandon the claim that sense observation is the source of all knowledge."
	- pp. 12-13, Radnitzky & Bartley, "Evolutionary Epistemology,
	Rationality, and the Sociology of Knowledge," Open Court
	publishers, 1987

This would seem to indicate authoritative approval of some other kind
of foundationalism, which might possibly include the variant developed
by David Kelley in "The Evidence of the Senses: a Realist Theory of
Perception."

My suggestion would be to describe extropianism as inclusive of
diverse methods of acquiring knowledge, perhaps indicating what they
all seem to have in common - most notably being at the frontiers of
the discipline, and that some method of some sort is preferred to
short-cuts such as special revelation.

