From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Subject: FAQ: Extropian Epistemology

Tim Starr writes: <I think it's a bad idea to commit extropianism to
pan-critical rationalism (or any other epistemology) to the exclusion of all
others for reasons both general and specific.>

Agreed.  In general, fledgling philosophies need maximum room to maneuver.
Premature pronouncements of dogma can marginalize them in a backwater quickly.
The central conceptual peaks should be pointed towards but the foothills around
the peaks should be left to creative forces.

Tim: <...committing extropianism to any current view runs a high risk of
making it obsolete if and when the state of the art advances.  I would
object to committing extropianism to physics theories such as those
of Einstein's theories which currently seem correct for the same reason...>

Agreed.  There is no finality in epistemological theories or theories of
physics.

	Coherency theories of epistemology serve an open-system, evolving
philosophy far better than foundationalist theories do.  Not that
foundationalism doesn't have merit, but as you say, there seems to be
foundations, then we discover sub-foundations, then more sub-foundations, and so
on.  Coherency theories inherently incorporate boundless expansion and
spontaneous order through the conceptual linkages shifting, realigning and
growing through time.  Indeed, foundationalism can be placed within coherency
theory as one concept that links with many other concepts, all hanging together.
The ability to subsume foundationalism within coherency theory makes coherency
theory a deeper foundation.  The biggest advantage of coherency theory is that
over time, the processes of consensual rationality can recognize the superiority
of the entire framework of extropian concepts hanging together compared to any
other framework.

