Subject: Punishment

Stuart Card makes an important point:

> I am not saying that I ALWAYS escalate.  In fact, I usually
> de-escalate (ignore provocation, or merely respond verbally).
> But I reserve the 'right' to escalate, and advertise the fact
> that I very well may do so, as a deterrent.
> 
> And if I were feeling sufficiently oppressed, I would trade
> an unhappy unfree life for death in battle.

It's the _apparent willingness_ to escalate, do battle, etc. that
often _heads off_ such battles. 

In the "game of chicken," which shows up in animal fighting displays,
in inter-nation standoffs (e.g., U.S.-Russia in Cuban missile crisis),
and even in criminal court proceeedings, this phenomenon is very
clearly shown.

The side which appears to be backing down, wimping out, muttering "Why
can't we all just get along?," etc., gets nuked, mugged, prosecuted,
etc. The side which threatens escalation, a strong defensee, a
willingness to fight back, even a kind of craziness, often wins by
default as the other side backs down.

And the _appearance of craziness_, or perhaps the appearance of
"irrationality," is often important. If you can show that you are
willing to in fact drive over the cliff (as in the "Rebel Without a
Cause" game of chicken), then you may not have to, as your opponent
will chicken out, meekly sneaking off. The key is not to actually go
over the cliff, only to make your opponent _think_ you are crazy
enough to do so.

The threat of "massive retaliation," however "icky" it may be to
wimp-simps, is often what keeps the peace.


--Tim May. whose MMGE has massive retaliation as its core prinicple.


