From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: If Uploads Come First

Robin Hanson raises some interesting points in his excellent If Uploads
Come First essay.  Taking some of his comments out of order:

> We should also expect an evolution of values regarding death and risk.
> Imagine situations in which making a copy [of an Unloader] might pay
> off big, but most likely the copy would fail, run out of money and
> have to be "evicted" from its brain and body.  Many folks might
> decline any such opportunities, because they so dislike the prospect
> of such "death".

Count me in on this!

> Others might consider this not much bigger a deal than forgetting what
> happened at a party because they were too drunk; "they" would only
> lose their experiences since the last copy event.  We expect evolution
> to prefer the later set of values over the former.

Indeed.  I expect uploadees would feel an attachment to close copies -
much like sibling loyalty and support, as we've previously discussed on
the list.  An assault on one is an assault on all.  Those upload copies
that form are bound together by some sort of kin-protection
agreement/understanding will have extra clout in their dealing with
others.  A cavalier disregard of personal death and avenging siblings
would make them valuable allies and formidable enemies.

> What would values evolve to? .... We expect that over many
> generations (i.e., times copied) Darwinian selection should favor
> maximum long-term generation of "wealth" that can be used to buy new
> copies.  That is, since upload reproduction can be so
> directly bought, we expect evolution to favor uploads whose values
> induce them to take actions which give their copy lineage the maximum
> financial return on their investments, including their investments in
> new copies or in "leisure."  (footnote: Regarding investment risk, I
> suspect evolution would favor maximizing the expected log return.)

I suspect the reverse: that evolution will favour the evolution of
uploads that value maximising expected return, not expected log return.

Here's an example.  A number of uploads in the same family are faced
with a fixed investment strategy.  Funds invested are returned either
two-fold (ie 200% return plus ante) or get wiped out, with equal
probability.  The decision each upload investor takes is deciding what
proportion to invest and what proportion to hold back.  Furthermore, for
simplicity, I'll imagine that each upload has mortgaged its own future
services to the limit to raise the investment capital.  An upload that
goes broke gets wiped out in more ways than one, having to sell
ownership of itself (become a slave to its bankers) to redeem its
borrowings.  An upload that doubles it capital can afford to (and will,
we'll suppose) create a copy of itself or buy a sibling out of slavery,
endowing it with the same financial security.

Uploads seeking to maximise log return will each invest 1/4 of their
personal fortune, keeping 3/4 in liquid form.  Half of the investments
are lost. The other half are successful and the value of these funds
triples.  Total financial assets owned by the upload family per
investment cycle increases by 9/8.  In 6 cycles we expect size of the
family to double.

Uploads seeking to maximise return will each invest _all_ of their
personal fortune.  Half of the investments are lost the owning uploads
get wiped out.  The other half are successful and the value of these
funds triples, enabling them to redeem their unfortunate siblings and
create more copies.  Total financial assets owned by the upload family
per investment cycle increases by 3/2.  It takes less than 2 cycles for
this family to double in size.

"Reckless" uploads that value maximising linear return have a very 
strong competitive advantage over the more "cowardly" uploads who only 
seek to maximise log return.  Over 30 investment cycles the linear 
