Subject: REPUTATION/LIBEL/FRAUD

> the use of libel laws to protect one's reputation.  For years, I
> have seen a parade of libertarian types totally misunderstanding
> the "fraud" aspect of libel/slander.

As an individualist, I'd appreciate if you didn't commit the common
net.fallacy of using my statements as a substitute for a bunch of
stuff I didn't write and probably didn't see. Some of my worst
net.arguments have come from this error.

Let's suppose a consultant named Alice is making a good living
teaching Transhuman Software Methodology; and further that Bob, who is
barely getting by teaching seminars on Historical Trends in Software
Methodism publishes a series of attack adds which paint TSM as the
ultimate in retrogressive evil, but without including Actual Fact #1.

Who has violated a libertaran tenet? Certainly Bob has, by committing
fraud; the advertising media have suffered a loss of credibility and
should have had language in their Ad Acceptance Form, sign here
please, creating a penalty for false and libelous utterance, or at
least disclaiming same, depending on how credible they want to be.

The culpable aspect of fraud is _in the utterance_, not in believing
what is said. How could there ever be an objective finding of damages
in favor of Carol, who bought a Mercury Villager instead of a Chrysler
minivan (I forget the model name, but someone recently told me it was
a terrific van) based on Alice's statements against Chrysler?  She
defamed Chrysler Corp., not Carol.

If Extropia is going to throw common sense and common law out the
window in favor of absolutist views of freedom, as in freedom to do
harm, then I'll have to trope another topia.

