Date: Sun, 9 Jan 1994 00:13:19 -0500
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Children as Property

> In this view, you own your own life, but your parents stick you
> with a bill for child-rearing at the time you are old enough to
> contract.  
 
 In a free society, one shouldn't have a right to bill others for
the services they never freely contracted for.
When you kids are *mature* (not *old*) enough to contract *freely*
(they may be smart enough to contract earlier, but since, due to
lack of experience/education/skills, they are still vitally dependent
on you, they are not really _free_ to contract), you can offer them
*new* contracts which you may bill them for (now parents do lend money
to their adult children, and nobody has a problem with it).
  Until your child matures, whatever services you provide may be
considered a free gift. If you don't like it - do not rear children.
However, if you brought a new sentient being into a life and put it
into a dependent position without asking its opinion, IMO you have
a certain obligation, and it would be immoral to make them suffer by
abandoning them or extorting money or promises when they are not yet
free to choose.
  Sure, parents can *ask* children to help them...  And if they did a
good job as parents, the children will be wealthy and loving enough
to help them; if not, the parents don't deserve the help anyway.
 
----------------------------------------------------------------
  
  As for the general issue of 'sentient property'...
 
  Conventional human ethics assigns "1 [undifferentiated] unit of ethical
value" to every member of Homo 'Sapiens', regardless of any features,
shows a bit of care to a vaguely defined collection of cute 'live' objects,
and considers the rest as 'things'.
 
  This approach is grossly inadequate.
There is an enormously complex [potential] realm of all kinds of entities
with everchanging features (there are relatively few *stable* entities
possible; all of them I consider *dead*), without any distinct borders,
and still more possible *arbitrary* ethical systems.
I personally wouldn't consider beings sharing the theory described in the
previous paragraph really 'sentient'...   Lots of problems in handling
crime, etc. arise from considering [creatures who behave like] animals
'human'...  Democracy with its  'one body, one vote' still follows the
distribution of the biomass - not sensitivity, interests, intelligence
or wealth.  However, I find these choices about as arbitrary as weight...
 
  There is going to be a lot of struggle over alternative ethical systems
as more and more of the potentiality of the entity realm gets actualized.
And I expect that the sides will base their 'opinions' on their interests
rather than any consistent theories.
 
 I remember suggesting the <>-abortionists who were debating the rights
of women for abortion as their rights over their bodies, to consider the
case when they would be *laying eggs* - thus separating moral obligation
issues from those of body ownership.
(I can send my messages on abortion to anybody interested, and would like
to read any *non-trivial* ideas on it.)
Neither of the sides had any interest in discussing philosophical premises
of the whole debate - only in shouting at their opponents.
   I wrote that I would prefer to deal with beings whose ethical values do
not depend on the pecularities of their reproduction techniques, and
got out of there...
 
 
-- Sasha?
 
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
  On fetuses:     _Potential_ humans deserve _potential_ rights...
  On potentials:  with good enough technology, every entity is potentially
                  <could be turned into> anything else. With a probable loss
                  of _identity_...
  On identity:    'Identity' is a funny term born from a mix of English,
                   Judeo-Christianic tradition and vulgar application of
                   binary logic to 'sentient' entities.  Arbitrary, highly
                   subjective and largely irrelevant.
