They share something in common: they are all wrong in long-term.
In a few cases when they are more or less accurate, they are recent
(i.e. short-term), and the parts they describe correctly are *not*
on the current frontier of development, but rather in the more
traditional realm.
Like population_growth/pollution vs. Internet development/designer_babies.
 
   With the structural change quickening, the time span in which our
forecasts may have any relevance, may be expected only to shrink
(as it has been so far).
 
   I would be very happy to hear arguments why our forecasts may be
expected to penetrate much more future historical turbulence than anybody
else's ever did.
 
